!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/schema/invalid/an-enum-over-a-family-that-is-not-text-expected.tn"
!!schema:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §7.4, [TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10"
  description: """
    An enum's members are labels, equal only to themselves under their label type's equality, and the
    kernel's `enum_of<T>` takes that label type. `T` must be a text family -- an atom-family instance
    whose constructor IS-A `text_type` -- so a meta layer applying it to `integer` is refused at
    schema load. A numeric value set is `integer`'s own `members` facet (`!integer ^ { members: [80 443] }`),
    where `80` and `0x50` are one member; as labels they would be two.

    The refusal belongs at the application. Without it `enum_of<integer>` resolves, and a governed
    schema's `!int_enum [1 2 3]` fails later, inside whatever binds the enum's members as text.
    """
  meta: "meta-kernel.tn"
  import: ["meta.tn"]
  error: {
    category: resolver
  }
}
